WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.883
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.883 --> 00:00:09.575
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hello, everybody.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:12.523
&gt;&gt; Leonard, thank you for
being here today.

00:00:12.523 --> 00:00:14.370
Welcome back to the GSV.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.122
&gt;&gt; It's always fun to be here.

00:00:16.122 --> 00:00:18.568
It's a lot nicer than when I was here.

00:00:18.568 --> 00:00:19.939
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes,

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:23.451
I've been hearing that quite a bit today,
given the previous here.

00:00:23.451 --> 00:00:25.974
I, I'm thrilled to be
having this conversation.

00:00:25.974 --> 00:00:30.830
I was told that you scheduled
your time in 15 minute slots.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.878
So we are taking up four
of those slots right now.

00:00:33.878 --> 00:00:35.590
So, I'll get right to it.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.190
I have a few questions here,
focusing on three things.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.431
Your leadership and management journey,
your thoughts of investing and

00:00:41.431 --> 00:00:45.656
entrepreneurship and then a few questions
on your personal life and legacy.

00:00:45.656 --> 00:00:49.270
After that, we'll take a few questions
from audience and from Twitter.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:52.010
As Dean Saloner mentioned,
you were the founder and

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.770
the first CEO at Sun Microsystems.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.032
About a year after you had started,

00:00:57.032 --> 00:01:01.722
you found out that Sun Microsystems was
gonna be shut out from a key thing.

00:01:01.722 --> 00:01:04.710
When you heard this, you got on a flight.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.888
Got to the customer's lobby and

00:01:06.888 --> 00:01:11.172
made phone calls refusing to leave
until you met with the team.

00:01:11.172 --> 00:01:14.100
Where does this perseverance and
conviction come from?

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.904
&gt;&gt; Well so, I have a philosophy in life.

00:01:19.904 --> 00:01:23.540
Whatever I believe, I should make happen.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.538
It's that simple.

00:01:25.538 --> 00:01:33.090
And if a customer's making a mistake,
[LAUGH] the customer isn't always right.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:33.759
Trust me.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:37.757
[LAUGH] And if you want to do great
products, don't listen to your customers,

00:01:37.757 --> 00:01:38.800
by the way.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.671
Lots of bad lessons in business
school I used to give a talk in

00:01:43.671 --> 00:01:47.822
the 80s on why not to listen
to your customers and

00:01:47.822 --> 00:01:53.079
maybe somebody will ask me a question
that story is in fact true.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:56.743
There was a signed contract
with a competitor,

00:01:56.743 --> 00:02:01.689
I stayed in the lobby all day
at 6 o'clock in the evening and

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:05.860
I'd taken a red eye out and
I parked in the lobby.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.366
I met with the CEO of the company,
mostly, because he wanted me to go away.

00:02:10.366 --> 00:02:15.047
By 9 o'clock, he had I'd convinced him to
meet me in Chicago with his team the next

00:02:15.047 --> 00:02:18.299
day, because he didn't wanna
be seen negotiating with us.

00:02:19.524 --> 00:02:22.940
The following morning,
the day after at 5 a.m.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.570
We signed a handwritten contract,
because I wouldn't let him leave.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:27.955
&gt;&gt; Oh.
&gt;&gt; This was the CEO for

00:02:27.955 --> 00:02:31.240
big East Coast company, Computer Vision.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.356
We signed a handwritten contract and
life was good.

00:02:35.356 --> 00:02:39.053
The point is if you
actually believe something,

00:02:39.053 --> 00:02:42.090
you try your best to make it happen.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.192
It doesn't always happen, but
it happens most of the time.

00:02:46.192 --> 00:02:49.820
Most of the business school
entry story is also true.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.464
I actually learned that
somebody had dropped out.

00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:59.745
That's why I showed up and one of the nice
ladies in the business school office,

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:06.396
put me in her living room to her husband's
chagrin and she was real nice to me.

00:03:06.396 --> 00:03:09.337
But things do workout if you persist.

00:03:09.337 --> 00:03:14.254
Not always, but I like to say,
failure does not matter,

00:03:14.254 --> 00:03:17.180
it's success that matters.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.472
And nobody remembers what you failed at.

00:03:21.472 --> 00:03:24.140
So, everybody remembers Sun.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:29.386
Does anybody know a company I
started before Sun that McNealy and

00:03:29.386 --> 00:03:31.142
I started together?

00:03:31.142 --> 00:03:31.820
Any hands?

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.842
&gt;&gt; I do, but
I've been researching you for awhile now.

00:03:35.842 --> 00:03:38.078
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What do you think the company is?

00:03:38.078 --> 00:03:38.970
&gt;&gt; Was it Data Dump?

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:39.610
Or was it?
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.182
There was a company called Data Dump,
we started and

00:03:43.182 --> 00:03:45.626
go funded three months before Sun.

00:03:45.626 --> 00:03:46.320
Didn't workout.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.528
We started both roughly together, but

00:03:50.528 --> 00:03:54.657
my point is people don't
remember your failures.

00:03:54.657 --> 00:04:00.636
And I like to say my willingness to fail
is what gives me the ability to succeed.

00:04:00.636 --> 00:04:05.724
I find most people are so
afraid of failing, they don't try and

00:04:05.724 --> 00:04:09.852
do the things that would
be important enough to do.

00:04:09.852 --> 00:04:14.152
Anything worth doing is hard an so.

00:04:14.152 --> 00:04:18.580
&gt;&gt; You've spoken in the past about the
importance of failure or the lack of it.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:21.362
You started your first
company at age 20 in Delhi,

00:04:21.362 --> 00:04:23.268
which did not work out as expected.

00:04:23.268 --> 00:04:25.459
&gt;&gt; Well, I didn't actually get started.

00:04:25.459 --> 00:04:26.007
&gt;&gt; Huh.

00:04:26.007 --> 00:04:29.440
&gt;&gt; They told me,
it'd be seven years to get a phone line.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.572
&gt;&gt; How did you react to that?

00:04:30.572 --> 00:04:34.514
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Honest to goodness.

00:04:34.514 --> 00:04:37.465
You know, I was 19 or 20 and they told me,

00:04:37.465 --> 00:04:40.820
it would be a 7 year waiting
list to get a land line.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.980
I, I, I sort of picked up and
went to Carnegie Mellon.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:51.785
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How was that like, the move to the US?

00:04:51.785 --> 00:04:54.420
&gt;&gt; You know, look, it, it was easy.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.750
I wanted to be in Silicon Valley,
I knew I'd get here.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.433
I had no way to pay for it, so

00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:02.590
I had to figure out how to
get somebody to pay for it.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.648
In the end, the Biomedical Engineering
Department at CMU agreed to pay for

00:05:08.648 --> 00:05:09.992
it, so I went there.

00:05:09.992 --> 00:05:13.110
And I was very impressed
in biomedical engineering.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.289
So, I did that and
then applied immediately to Stanford and

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:18.013
you heard the rest of the story.

00:05:18.013 --> 00:05:22.593
They turned me down a couple of times,
but, you know,

00:05:22.593 --> 00:05:26.378
the funny thing is with
enough persistence,

00:05:26.378 --> 00:05:31.215
most things that seem
impossible become possible.

00:05:31.215 --> 00:05:36.876
I'm always amazed at how often
you can turn things around and

00:05:36.876 --> 00:05:40.650
people take no for an answer too easily.

00:05:41.972 --> 00:05:46.869
You know, it's whether you're trying
to achieve a goal like get into

00:05:46.869 --> 00:05:50.693
business school or my,
my green card was the same way.

00:05:50.693 --> 00:05:54.539
I left the job that had sponsored me,
but I still got my green card,

00:05:54.539 --> 00:05:57.380
even though no lawyer would take me.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.004
I became my own lawyer.

00:05:59.004 --> 00:06:02.574
Got my green card without working for,
while I was in business school.

00:06:02.574 --> 00:06:05.870
[LAUGH] And I did it, perfectly, legally.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.542
I, my strategy there was if I
couldn't convince immigration,

00:06:09.542 --> 00:06:11.179
I'd confuse them and I did.

00:06:11.179 --> 00:06:13.164
[LAUGH] And they gave me my green card.

00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:16.613
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:06:16.613 --> 00:06:19.666
you know, I, I just wanna ask you,
what are some,

00:06:19.666 --> 00:06:23.800
some of the perhaps, the early
influences that made you this way?

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.330
This high perseverance,
high conviction person.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:31.600
&gt;&gt; Well I, I was sort of always this way.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.699
I didn't grow up in this environment,

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:36.890
nobody around my dad was in
the Indian arm since he was 16.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:40.374
So he joined the ranks of
the Indian Army in, when he was 16,

00:06:40.374 --> 00:06:43.202
got shipped off to Egypt to fight for
the British.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:47.840
And so,
I grew up in a very different environment.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.953
But I always had this view that if
I determined something was right,

00:06:52.953 --> 00:06:55.024
I was gonna make it happen.

00:06:55.024 --> 00:06:59.110
And this comes to, since one of
the focuses of this lecture is leadership.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:04.136
I am amazed at how few
people have a belief system.

00:07:04.136 --> 00:07:05.809
What do they actually believe in?

00:07:07.704 --> 00:07:13.478
I and, and, yeah,
it's okay to offend you guys.

00:07:13.478 --> 00:07:19.510
[LAUGH] 90% of you will do what's expected
of you as opposed to what you wanna do.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:25.572
And, you know, I even, when I come to
an event like this say, why am I here?

00:07:25.572 --> 00:07:30.421
Why would I even bother to take
an hour of my valuable time?

00:07:30.421 --> 00:07:32.490
[LAUGH] no, it's true.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.005
I, I, I have to have a purpose.

00:07:35.005 --> 00:07:36.210
My goal is very simple.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:41.158
If I can convert one of you,
there's probably 400, 500 people here.

00:07:41.158 --> 00:07:46.408
If I can convert one of you to follow
your belief and have the guts to follow

00:07:46.408 --> 00:07:51.155
your belief, I'll have,
I'll think of the hour as well spent.

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:52.456
Right?

00:07:52.456 --> 00:07:54.639
So, I would say,
what am I trying to do and why?

00:07:54.639 --> 00:08:00.097
I don't speak, because somebody you know,
will write nice things about it.

00:08:00.097 --> 00:08:04.600
I, I always sort of have a purpose,
but I have a belief system.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:10.470
And most people,
even successful companies,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:15.430
and I have big beef with Fortune 500
CEOs I can talk about, there's very little

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:20.910
leadership there don't have an internal
compass of what they believe in.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.620
Its what do others expect?

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.613
You know when I graduated,
I went into entrepreneurship.

00:08:26.613 --> 00:08:31.620
Nobody went into entrepreneurship
out of the business school.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:35.070
And people said why wouldn't you work for
Goldman or McKenzie.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.290
And I said why would you.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.220
I, I couldn't fathom why you
would wanna do that job.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:43.840
Alright?

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.440
I still can't, by the way.

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:52.490
For those of you going to those places,
and

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:56.050
I'm sure there's plenty, I'm probably
not the right person to talk to you.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.230
I frankly don't even care to talk to you.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.055
All of you others.

00:09:01.055 --> 00:09:05.050
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:07.960
&gt;&gt; I think there's something wrong
with anybody who fundamentally

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.870
wants to go work for
one of the consultants or

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:12.122
the investment banks today and
there's probably.

00:09:12.122 --> 00:09:13.740
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That, this,

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:17.840
they don't understand how the world
is going to work in the future.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:20.800
But my point again is,
have a belief system.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.640
Follow that compass.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.350
And I'm overstating it a bit, so there may
be some hope for those of you going there.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:32.400
But it's a li,
only a little bit of an exaggeration.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.890
Let me go back to this
issue of Fortune 500 CEOs.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:39.700
I've talked to many over the years.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.680
If a New York Times
writer writes an article,

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:47.260
they want to change their strategy,
they wanna respond.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.860
How silly is that, that some English
major who's never had a business job

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.390
can determine the strategy
of a Fortune 500 company?

00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:59.630
Why?
Because they care about what's written,

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.120
not what their belief system is.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:05.460
Do you know what Elon Musk
would say to that writer?

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.370
Go get a real job.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:09.140
All right.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.210
What Jeff Bezos would say?

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:12.440
That's leadership.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.470
Having a belief system.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.380
Knowing what's driving
you to do what you do.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:19.910
Not you're fitting in to
do career enhancement.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:25.300
Or, making it look good to somebody else.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:31.368
Whether it's to your friends, or
to your boss, or to shareholders.

00:10:31.368 --> 00:10:36.720
And frankly, you look at all the companies
that are languishing in the Valley,

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.980
the older companies,
they generally don't have a belief system.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:45.190
They're generally trying to meet some
quarterly target, without saying,

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:48.380
here's how we will be different and
here's how we'll do it.

00:10:49.988 --> 00:10:54.560
Apple did languish for 10,
15 years, and then Steve Jobs came

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:58.940
in with a point of view, and created
the most valuable company in the world.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:04.460
He did irrational things,
if you go back to January of 2007,

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:09.360
January 1, everybody was saying nobody
wants a phone without a keyboard.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.420
Nobody wants a phone that costs 699.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.570
I can go on with the litany.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.270
First of all,
English majors writing articles,

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:19.495
I have this beef against English majors.

00:11:19.495 --> 00:11:22.970
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Not all English majors are bad,

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:27.850
but the majority of them at Stanford
are undirected and don't have a goal or

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:29.266
a purpose.

00:11:29.266 --> 00:11:32.123
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:11:32.123 --> 00:11:38.040
And,and that's not to say among them
there isn't 20% that are really good.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:41.665
They just didn't get the right guidance
and ended up in the wrong place.

00:11:41.665 --> 00:11:46.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I like proper school education.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:46.620
Anyway, sorry.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:47.240
Back to you.

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:47.845
&gt;&gt; No, absolutely.

00:11:47.845 --> 00:11:48.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is,

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.182
this actually is an interesting
&gt;&gt; I make up better questions than usually

00:11:52.182 --> 00:11:52.981
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:11:52.981 --> 00:11:54.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's a challenge

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:55.625
for you guys.

00:11:55.625 --> 00:11:57.440
#GSVBFTT.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.550
But I want to ask you about this,
you are known for being for

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.535
the lack of a better word somewhat blunt.

00:12:02.535 --> 00:12:04.950
[LAUGH]
But

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.720
your website says we prefer brutal
honesty to hypocritical politeness.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.260
What are the tradeoffs in doing this?

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:13.700
&gt;&gt; Well there's lots of tradeoffs but.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:17.660
I'm, I'm again very clear.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:20.670
You know it started off
earlier in my life.

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:25.520
I was very successful, even before
I started Sun, I started a company

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:30.370
like Daisy that made me more money, which
was only a million dollars back then,

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:34.880
or little more than that
than my dad had made in the,

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.670
in his career cumulatively, so
I was in a whole different place.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:44.230
And I said, the one thing I'll do is
not do what others want me do, or

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:47.100
be polite,
which generally means dishonest.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:52.250
There's a great book by Sam Harris,

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:55.590
who's a professor over on the other side,
about lying.

00:12:55.590 --> 00:13:00.230
I recommend everybody read
Sam Harris' book on lying, and

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.990
why we all lie too much and too often.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:10.710
We put this sort of slogan,
I prefer brutal honesty to hypocritical

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:15.950
politeness, on our website,
because it's a real disservice to people.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.740
You don't have to be
offensive to be honest.

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:21.240
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:26.090
&gt;&gt; When I don't like somebody,
I can get offensive, too.

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:31.340
But generally you can be constructive and
very honest.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:35.710
And I prefer that brutal honesty,
because it serves a purpose.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:41.110
The receiver can do something about it,
if it's a constructive criticism.

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:48.140
If you get po, hypocritical politeness,
you might lose a lot.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:52.640
So, again,
it's about having enough confidence to say

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:56.030
the other person will eventually
appreciate it, and that loc,

00:13:56.030 --> 00:14:00.810
local goods-slogan came after I
was working on a company with

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:04.826
another major rental firm, and
I didn't believe their plan would work.

00:14:04.826 --> 00:14:09.010
They had $45,
$50 million in their bank account,

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.640
so everybody was comfortable,
too comfortable, in my view.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:14.760
I talk to the founders
why it wouldn't work.

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:17.780
What my concerns were.

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:23.310
The other VCs agreed with me in the board
meeting, said all these polite things.

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:29.690
You know, so 40 engineers wasted three or
four years of their life because

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:33.340
nobody was willing to tell them the honest
truth about what they really thought.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:38.490
Right and I swore I would never
let anybody waste their life.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:45.100
At least give them the best advice I had
to give and tell them I may be wrong,

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:49.420
but I'm not gonna say something I
don't believe in, I just won't.

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:55.400
In, and that's extremely
valuable to the other thing.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:15:00.220
But to be honest, look, there
are situations you, it offends people, and

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:07.030
I offend people often, but it helps
them if they're really introspective.

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:07.830
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:08.900
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:12.820
I just had a candidate from Howard
Business School come in and interview for

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:16.250
a job and I,
half an hour into the meeting I said,

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.980
you know, you're not a match for us.

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:22.200
Here's what I would do, and
I spent the next half hour.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:26.830
I said, I literally said to her, if I'm
gonna waste the next half hour of my life

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:31.190
talking to you, I'm gonna give you my
best advice on what to do about your

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:32.320
career,
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:33.640
&gt;&gt; which I did.

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:34.880
She didn't love it.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.090
She sent me a nice note a week later.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.990
But its very valuable.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:47.534
But the real fact,
the honest truth about why I like that is,

00:15:47.534 --> 00:15:51.832
once I was in a position
I didn't need a job.

00:15:51.832 --> 00:15:53.040
Nobody could fire me.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.131
I've actually never worked for anybody,
so it's actually sort of nice.

00:15:57.131 --> 00:16:02.940
I felt it was a indulgence or
a luxury I could enjoy.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:08.924
Some people, if they make, get that
financial freedom might buy a yacht.

00:16:08.924 --> 00:16:14.860
I bought myself the freedom of brutal
honesty, and it's an indulgence.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:18.220
I get to do things others may
not be in a position to do.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:23.500
There is a downside for others, but
I think generally constructive honesty and

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:28.370
constructive criticism than hypocritical
politeness will serve other people well.

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:35.040
That founder, by the way, in that company
called me four years later after,

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:39.610
after this company, this company got
sold for $3 million four, three and

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:41.443
a half, four years later.

00:16:41.443 --> 00:16:44.130
Sad story.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:46.475
And he called me a year after that.

00:16:46.475 --> 00:16:48.512
He said, I only wanna work for

00:16:48.512 --> 00:16:52.840
you on my next venture because
I wish somebody had told me.

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:59.950
&gt;&gt; You mentioned, you talked briefly about
venture capital as it stands now earlier.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:01.780
I just wanna come back to that.

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:06.454
Specifically, how does it compare to 1982,
when you were starting Sun Microsystems,

00:17:06.454 --> 00:17:08.130
and what are the major
differences you see?

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:11.590
&gt;&gt; It's a lot more popular now.

00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:12.840
It's a lot more accepted.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:16.250
It's a lot more accepted to do startups.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:21.260
So for those of you who are so inclined,
it's much easier to take that route.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:29.280
It wasn't back then, so the risk of going
the entrepreneurial route was much higher.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.010
If you went entrepreneurial and

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:37.050
it didn't work out you couldn't
get a job at GE, right?

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:38.340
Much harder.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:43.800
Today, if you've done a startup, GE would
love to have you, even if it failed.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:47.802
In fact, would prefer you over
somebody who spent two years at GE.

00:17:47.802 --> 00:17:50.770
Not 100% true.

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:54.740
But generally,
it's the learning experience of a startup,

00:17:54.740 --> 00:18:00.173
whether it's successful or unsuccessful,
is recognized as extremely valuable.

00:18:00.173 --> 00:18:07.570
And the cultural training of that is
something everybody's striving for.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:10.330
I'm amazed at the people who
come through Silicon Valley.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:15.070
It feels like a tourist stop,
like Fisherman's Wharf used to be.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:20.090
Every Fortune 500 CEO coming here,

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.780
holding board meetings here because
they wanna learn the culture.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:27.680
And, and I think that becomes
a real career enhancement for

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:29.116
those of you who do that.

00:18:29.116 --> 00:18:32.115
Now, I'm not a big fan
of planning careers.

00:18:32.115 --> 00:18:36.177
I'm much more about driving for
the things you wanna do and go do them.

00:18:36.177 --> 00:18:39.480
&gt;&gt; And, and that's about entrepreneurship.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:40.440
What about venture capital?

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:45.184
I mean, for instance, you very famously
said in a TechCrunch interview,

00:18:45.184 --> 00:18:49.958
95% venture capitalists add no value,
and 70% add negative value.

00:18:49.958 --> 00:18:52.674
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All of my venture capital friends love

00:18:52.674 --> 00:18:53.444
that statement.

00:18:53.444 --> 00:18:54.673
&gt;&gt; Yes, they do.

00:18:54.673 --> 00:18:56.574
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I, I, I tell them that, you know,

00:18:56.574 --> 00:18:57.447
there was a major firm.

00:18:57.447 --> 00:19:03.345
I called somebody there and said,
this guy's destroying this company, right?

00:19:03.345 --> 00:19:04.332
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:19:04.332 --> 00:19:04.911
&gt;&gt; It happens all the time.

00:19:04.911 --> 00:19:11.080
One, it, look venture capital's
become a big business.

00:19:12.918 --> 00:19:15.440
It didn't use to be back then.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:18.120
It was a specialty art form.

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.430
There was a few firms, and most of them.

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:24.750
Sequoia was started with Don Valentine.

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:27.610
If you know Don, he was an operator.

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:32.010
Kleiner was started by Tom Perkins and
Eugene Kleiner.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:37.114
Tom ran a division of HP, and
before that, he was a technologist.

00:19:37.114 --> 00:19:39.399
These guys, and, and
I'm not one for looking back and

00:19:39.399 --> 00:19:41.941
being romantic about things,
but this issue is important.

00:19:41.941 --> 00:19:47.760
These guys earned the right

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:53.853
to advise an entrepreneur.

00:19:53.853 --> 00:19:58.580
That means they'd gone through
the battles, they, they had the experience

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:02.740
when a unique situation came up and
an entrepreneur wanted advice.

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:08.710
They actually had been through
the firing line to viscerally understand

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:09.720
how to give them advice.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:17.300
Most VCs have not earned the right
to advise an entrepreneur, yet

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:19.350
they feel free to in their ignorance.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:26.210
And I believe you should have
gone through a startup or

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:30.490
at least some significant experiences, and
there are good exceptions to this rule,

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:34.730
to really advise entrepreneurs
on how to build a company.

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:40.000
And most VCs haven't done that,
so they give stupid advice.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.170
You know,
if you got out of business school and

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:44.180
joined a VC firm and grew up and

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:49.690
eventually became a partner, you haven't
learned what it's like to be in a startup.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:51.113
So in our firm, we have a rule.

00:20:51.113 --> 00:20:55.410
You can join, you join for
three to four years.

00:20:55.410 --> 00:21:00.280
Then you'll have to leave, actually work
in a startup, before you come back, and

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:05.110
actually can get, I'll give you the
responsibility to advise entrepreneurs.

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:10.745
People get frustrated because their peers
in other venture firms are sitting on

00:21:10.745 --> 00:21:13.240
boards and advising entrepreneurs.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:17.730
But, you know,
that's exercising authority as opposed to

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:21.167
exercising influence because you
have credibility with entrepreneurs.

00:21:21.167 --> 00:21:24.211
Most of the time I don't go on boards.

00:21:24.211 --> 00:21:27.440
I just advise entrepreneurs.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.050
And frankly,
being on a board is irrelevant.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:35.720
&gt;&gt; On that topic you have a really
unique strategy among VCs.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:38.510
You've said,
I'm only interested in technologies that

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:42.580
have a 90% chance of failure, but if they
do succeed would change the infrastructure

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:43.800
of society in a radical way.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.930
How did you come to adopt
this particular strategy?

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:47.750
&gt;&gt; Well, so,

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:51.910
that's not exactly accurate because
we don't mind low-risk technologies,.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:54.150
We invest in that, too.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:58.166
But what I wanna say is, venture capital,

00:21:58.166 --> 00:22:03.420
if you think about it, isn't,
is not an IRR business.

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:08.180
You, you,
you take any of the big successes.

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:10.710
Google, Facebook, Twitter.

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:11.830
You can't predict them.

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.910
You know, a camera like GoPro,
can you predict the, the market cap?

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:17.410
You can't.

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:24.850
And you can do spreadsheets, so we don't
allow any IRR calculations in our firm.

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:27.900
Nobody does them, nobody looks at them.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:30.650
In making investments,
we never calculate an IRR.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:34.780
Because calculating an IRR, in my view,

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:38.370
is creating the illusion of knowing,
which big companies are very good at.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:41.850
You tweak some assumption.

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:45.388
I did enough business plans to know I can
make the business plan do whatever I want

00:22:45.388 --> 00:22:46.631
on the spreadsheet.

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:49.630
You know, reality is different.

00:22:49.630 --> 00:22:54.210
I don't even think you can plan startups,
so business plans are largely irrelevant

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:00.570
other than in surfacing the key issues
the entrepreneur is thinking about.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:01.910
How thorough is their thinking?

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:03.810
What's the quality of their thinking?

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:06.170
That's the only thing I
use a business plan for.

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:09.420
Then what are you doing?

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:15.570
You're really doing option value
investing, if you want a financial term.

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:18.890
If you lose,
you lose one time with your money.

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:20.820
If you win, you win ten times your money.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:22.895
Your downside is limited
to your investment.

00:23:22.895 --> 00:23:26.120
So whenever we make an investment,
I assume we lost it, and

00:23:26.120 --> 00:23:27.505
then there's only upside.

00:23:27.505 --> 00:23:29.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.890
As soon as you write it off in your mind,
there's only upside.

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:36.620
So that's how I view the world.

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:39.140
And then you try and take the higher risk.

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:43.216
And there's VCs who won't invest
with us because they feel like we,

00:23:43.216 --> 00:23:45.480
we take too much risk, which is fine.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:49.988
There's room for every style of VC firm.

00:23:49.988 --> 00:23:51.687
Ours is different.

00:23:51.687 --> 00:23:54.566
It's about taking higher risks and
higher upside.

00:23:54.566 --> 00:23:59.568
90% chance of failure is not a problem.

00:23:59.568 --> 00:24:03.329
If there's a ten percent chance
of 100 times your money.

00:24:03.329 --> 00:24:09.340
If it's the only thing that worked,
then you're in pretty good shape.

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:14.170
And, and
that's not the only good strategy.

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:18.720
Private equity does very well
running spreadsheets and

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:21.800
that, I just have no
interest in spreadsheets.

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:23.070
So, and we're not good at it.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:24.850
&gt;&gt; Hm.
&gt;&gt; So we know what we do.

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:30.020
What's bad is when people who
come from that word want to do

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:31.230
this kind of investing.

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; It can't be a mismatch

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:35.390
of your personality with
the investing type.

00:24:36.710 --> 00:24:41.290
But all those are good scenarios and
some of them are even better scenarios,

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:44.760
for our art than what we do.

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:47.400
&gt;&gt; We, sorry.

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:48.750
You, you mentioned in your,

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:51.410
you mentioned right now about getting
other VC students to invest with you.

00:24:52.648 --> 00:24:55.541
And throughout your investing period you
had a strong streak of going against

00:24:55.541 --> 00:24:56.440
conventional wisdom.

00:24:57.464 --> 00:25:00.390
NexGen, Juniper I'm curious.

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:02.880
How do you get others to
buy into your convictions?

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:08.570
&gt;&gt; Sometimes they do after the fact,
sometimes they don't.

00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:10.920
Sometimes they actually
don't know any better.

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:12.178
So they're confused, which is good.

00:25:12.178 --> 00:25:15.150
[NOISE] A, all those happen.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:17.480
Look, there's lots of really good.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:20.946
Receives a lot, right?

00:25:20.946 --> 00:25:25.630
But there's order of magnitude
more bad we sees around.

00:25:27.270 --> 00:25:33.800
It's, so there's probably half a dozen
people I respect in the VC business and

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:38.110
probably the vast majority I don't
know personally so I won't judge them.

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:44.490
But of all the people I know in the VC
busni, 90% really add no value, and

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:52.020
I truly belive 70% of them reduce
the potential of a company.

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:54.425
&gt;&gt; I could, I could keep asking
you questions on investing, but

00:25:54.425 --> 00:25:56.835
I really wanna-
&gt;&gt; But, but, here's the thing, right?

00:25:56.835 --> 00:25:58.160
90% chance of failure.

00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:02.150
You have to have the courage to fail them,

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:06.640
because most likely you're going to,
to get that option at a very large play.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:12.670
Well, and, and
that's a pretty clear segment

00:26:12.670 --> 00:26:15.800
of the venture capital business or
the investing business.

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:17.590
It's not every segment.

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:22.250
Capital preservation is important in other
segments it's just boring to me, it's much

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:27.590
more exiting to be on a roller coaster
where the highs a high and a lows a low.

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:31.610
So risk taking is
absolutely essential now.

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:35.060
As career advice to all of you, most,

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:41.180
you can make money and good status,
and all that in regular big companies.

00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:45.580
There's very little really interesting
stuff you can do in big companies.

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:52.250
It comes shockingly,
it's shocking to people, but

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:54.620
who invented, reinvented retail?

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:55.794
It wasn't Wal-Mart.

00:26:57.150 --> 00:26:57.970
It was Amazon.

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:02.270
Who reinvented media it wasn't NBC.

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:03.490
It was YouTube.

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:07.510
Who reinvented space it wasn't Lockheed.

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:08.390
It was Space X.

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:14.930
I was hard pressed to think of
one thing that had come out

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:18.000
of a large company in the last 20,
30 years.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.560
That was materially
changing the landscape.

00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:28.620
And the reason's very simple, and
this is all interesting things have,

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:31.180
eh, happened at the edges of the system.

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:34.807
They don't happen in the solid core.

00:27:34.807 --> 00:27:39.860
And, all that

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:45.710
you have to do where things are fuzzy,
unclear, uncertain.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:50.680
If there's a market forecast,
it's not at the edge of the system.

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:52.450
If some analyst has a report.

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:57.770
It's not what's ex,
going into that area, right?

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:02.895
I've a whole thing about
analysts we can get there.

00:28:02.895 --> 00:28:05.546
&gt;&gt; [LAUGHTER]
&gt;&gt; Let's actually talk about it.

00:28:05.546 --> 00:28:08.027
&gt;&gt; Okay.
So ask me after I finish this.

00:28:08.027 --> 00:28:08.673
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:28:08.673 --> 00:28:09.173
&gt;&gt; Talk.

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:18.660
In the edges where things are uncertain
is they're old evolutionists and

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:23.410
there interesting changes in business or
society happen.

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:32.060
And unless you're there and
learning fast from being there and

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:37.150
not being too out of it to believe you
know what's going to happen you're not

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:42.570
gonna be in that learning edge of
the wave, where new things are happening.

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:44.220
And as career advice, I say.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:46.810
And that's where failure happens often.

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:51.660
You want failure to be small,
and success to be very large,

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:54.440
which is the characteristic
of option value.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:59.730
You lose your option price, but
you get option value if it's successful.

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.785
That all happens at the edge.

00:29:02.785 --> 00:29:07.130
In automotive, I, as, th,th,
there was a CEO, in fact,

00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:11.950
I've had three CEOs from the ten
largest auto companies come by in

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:16.750
the last six months and I said I wouldn't
worry about building a better car.

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:22.820
Almost certainly,
ten years from now, a Volkswagen or

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:26.440
a Toyota will be a financing entity,
not a car-making entity.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:29.730
There will not be the notion
of marketing to consumers.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:35.830
The auto industry as we know
it I think will disappear.

00:29:35.830 --> 00:29:39.720
Disappearing in the sense of IBM
mainframes are still around, they're still

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:44.220
a business, they're relatively static,
they're filling an old need.

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:48.310
But almost certainly, the number of
cards produced ten years from now will

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:52.870
be less than half of what is produced
today for sale to consumers.

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:53.600
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:58.280
&gt;&gt; I, now, long, we don't have enough
time to go into why I believe that.

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:02.260
Almost certainly, and
I'm serious about this.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:05.990
I gave a talk at the Stanford med school,
and I said,

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:11.950
if I wanted to be a good doctor in 15,
20 years, I wouldn't go to the med school.

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:15.620
If I wanted to be a good doctor,
I would go to the math department.

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:18.380
Almost certainly,

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:24.040
every single thing they're learning about
medicine will be obsolete in 15 years.

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:30.490
So, you can join Medtronic, or
DE Medical, and do incremental things, or

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:35.430
you can try to learn that the edges, where
your probability of failure is much high.

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:39.870
But the consequences of success
are really consequential, and

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:44.910
most people in their life,
in their business, reduce risk

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:49.130
to the point where the probability
of success goes higher, but

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:52.710
the consequences of success
are inconsequential,

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:58.260
as opposed to reducing the probability
of success and increasing

00:30:58.260 --> 00:31:03.660
the consequences of success.which
is sort of my life to start with.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:08.596
Trust me, it's a lot more fun
then spending 30 years and

00:31:08.596 --> 00:31:11.490
Goldman and I like Goldman.

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:14.110
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's a fascinating world
view, but I really want to get a question.

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:15.810
&gt;&gt; So let me go back to.

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:18.540
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:20.490
&gt;&gt; I, I, I just have seen the tide.

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:23.930
But you did ask me to talk about
&gt;&gt; The analysts and the experts.

00:31:23.930 --> 00:31:24.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
The experts.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:34.380
So Professor Chetlark when he was at
Berkley did research on expert opinion.

00:31:34.380 --> 00:31:39.400
All the analysts you read all
the Mackenzie reports, and

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.790
by the way, Bain and PCG no different.

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:47.686
And he followed all of their forecasts for
20 years,

00:31:47.686 --> 00:31:52.900
28,000 individual
forecasts from 250 experts.

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:55.349
Where when the forecast was made,

00:31:55.349 --> 00:32:00.162
he classified what would be a success,
what would be a failure, And

00:32:00.162 --> 00:32:06.158
the book is called "Expert Political
Judgement." So, all these great studies,

00:32:06.158 --> 00:32:10.718
the 10-year studies McKenzie did for
millions of dollars for

00:32:10.718 --> 00:32:16.380
AT&amp;T on what would happen to the cell
phone business, that's a fun story.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:23.974
Across 28,000 individual forecast
in 84,000 possible scenarios.

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:31.185
The average accuracy of the best experts,
whether it was Henry Kissinger,

00:32:31.185 --> 00:32:37.810
Tom Friedman, McKenzie, your favorite,
eh, favorite economic forecaster.

00:32:37.810 --> 00:32:42.734
The average accuracy,
was about the same, to use his words,

00:32:42.734 --> 00:32:44.920
as dart throwing monkeys.

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:49.650
So if you want to run your
life on these forecast and

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.000
create an illusion of knowing.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:52.080
Great, go do it!

00:32:53.402 --> 00:32:58.154
You will follow a path that others
will reinforce because you can

00:32:58.154 --> 00:33:01.680
give them a data quest study or
a Bain forecast.

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:06.490
Or you can discover the edges where
things are happening and changing.

00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:12.460
Know that you don't know,
and really change the world.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:18.630
Now, again bringing back to leadership,
it's about having a point of view.

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:21.450
It's about having an internal compass.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:24.000
This is a great point in your life.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.220
To actually decide which one of those.

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:28.080
Do you have a belief system?

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:30.570
Or you gonna do what
others tell you to do?

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:33.420
Whether it's written up
in the New York Times,

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:38.350
which, which somebody wrote up who
doesn't understand the context.

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:41.310
Whether it's the McKenzie report or
a G-Forecast, or

00:33:41.310 --> 00:33:44.588
a Goldman analyst, or
you gonna do what you believe in?

00:33:44.588 --> 00:33:51.850
And unfortunately,
very few of you will do that.

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:54.980
I hope I can work some of you
to believing in yourself.

00:33:56.970 --> 00:34:04.390
And the good news is,
I actually believe five percent of

00:34:04.390 --> 00:34:08.490
the people is all that's needed to be even
remotely innovative to change society.

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:14.750
I once looked at all social changes,
not just business,

00:34:15.950 --> 00:34:21.070
and I looked at a decade and I said for
people born between 1940 and

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:25.320
1950 or 1950 of all the people
born on this planet.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:28.300
You can take the ten ma, 100 major areas.

00:34:30.050 --> 00:34:34.670
And define, could you find 100
people in each of those areas,

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:38.990
from that decade, that generation,
who affected change?

00:34:38.990 --> 00:34:43.450
It didn't matter whether
it was music,arts,

00:34:43.450 --> 00:34:49.160
research in social sciences,
technology invention, business.

00:34:49.160 --> 00:34:51.670
You can't find a hundred areas and
you cannot

00:34:51.670 --> 00:34:56.840
find a hundred people that stand out in
each of these areas in any given decade.

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:01.809
So, less than 10,000 people
born this decade will influence

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:09.660
All of what society does think, happen.

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:14.590
I'd like to say you should try and
be one of those people, and

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:18.860
none of those people will
follow any analyst's forecast.

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:21.000
They will have an internal compass,

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:26.590
they'll have self-confidence in a belief
system, and they'll make change happen.

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:29.760
I'm very passionate about this,
cuz frankly,

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:35.430
it only takes a few people to change the
direction of any industry or social trend.

00:35:36.670 --> 00:35:39.890
&gt;&gt; You know, I think this might be a good
time to open up the floor to questions

00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:41.760
from the audience.

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:44.890
So we have Caroline and
Rai on either side with mics.

00:35:44.890 --> 00:35:48.240
Please keep your hands raised so
that one of them can spot you.

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:52.820
You can also tweet your questions
using #gsbvftt, and Michelle here

00:35:52.820 --> 00:35:56.060
will ask your question, actually lets
start with a question from twitter.

00:35:56.060 --> 00:36:00.280
&gt;&gt; And I want to apologize I have
to leave soon after this, but

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:05.670
my email address is vk@coastalventures.com
if we don't get to your question,

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:08.175
please feel free to send it.

00:36:08.175 --> 00:36:12.470
&gt;&gt; Thank you Vino-
&gt;&gt; And I'll spend as much time answering

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:16.455
it as, as much time as you spent
thoughtfully asking the question.

00:36:16.455 --> 00:36:20.670
[LAUGH]
That, that's my rule on email.

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:26.700
I try and judge how much, how thoughtful
was somebody in sending the email.

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:27.570
Yes, sorry.

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.280
&gt;&gt; Alright, there's a lot of very
thoughtful questions on Twitter regarding

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:31.740
your belief system.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:36.300
Can you talk about what exactly
is your core belief system, and

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:39.300
how do you foster that in your team and
your company?

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:46.160
&gt;&gt; You know, so I decided very early I
wanted to work on interesting things.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:54.360
Is not well known but I started the first
program, programming class at NEIIT.

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:59.160
In 1971, we formed a hobby club of
four people to learn programming, and

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:03.900
that was the first instance I know
of a programming class at NEIIT.

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:09.570
A few years later, I got interested
in biomedical engineering, and

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:14.160
I was 16 when I started the programming
club at IIT Delhi, a few

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:18.480
years later we started the biomedical
engineering program, which was, we just

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:22.830
convinced one professor to work with us,
and there was three or four of us, and so

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:26.769
I had this habit of saying, what do I
want to do and how to make it happen.

00:37:29.020 --> 00:37:33.020
But the core belief system I have is,
I wanna work on interesting things.

00:37:33.020 --> 00:37:33.990
I don't wanna get bored.

00:37:37.510 --> 00:37:39.410
And it applies to what I'm doing today.

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:45.870
What I've learned since is,
it's nice to do that, but

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:49.670
it's even more rewarding to do interesting
things that have a great impact.

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:54.210
And so I measure impact a lot.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.650
We are probably the only VC
firm that in the first, for

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:01.890
the first slide deck
a fundraising slide deck

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:06.060
we said we care about our ambition
which is R&amp;R but care as much but

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:10.890
more about our ambition, our mission
which is to make a positive change.

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:17.260
And, I've told my LPs if you get a lower
rate of return but higher impact,

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:21.710
we'll probably pick that, because I'm much
more interested in that than feeding some

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:26.690
pension fund a slightly higher rate
of return as long as I cross a bar.

00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:28.390
It's very clear to me.

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:31.650
So, it was cool stuff and
interesting stuff,

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:35.220
early I didn't have
perspective when I was in 20s.

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:39.640
And over time I said, it's much more fun
to work on things that make a difference.

00:38:39.640 --> 00:38:41.760
And so I only do that.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:46.400
We, we had an investment and somebody
will tweet this and I'll get in trouble.

00:38:47.610 --> 00:38:50.800
We are looking at investment in a company
called T Spraying which is doing

00:38:50.800 --> 00:38:51.436
extremely well.

00:38:51.436 --> 00:38:55.480
And the partner who was doing that,
and I said you know,

00:38:55.480 --> 00:38:59.360
you want to invest in T Spraying or
t-shirts, great.

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:01.100
Don't expect me to spend any time.

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:04.640
I'm not interested in t-shirts.

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:09.823
I'd rather work on a healthcare solution,
and so,

00:39:09.823 --> 00:39:15.530
it's not, again, I want to emphasize
this is a luxury I've earned.

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:20.000
So, all of you don't have that luxury,
if you're not in the same place, and

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:22.850
I recognize when other people
aren't in the same place.

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:25.760
I also never cared what people thought,

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:29.080
so I had that luxury
very early on in my life.

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:36.330
Mostly through my attitude, which was
much more belligerent when I was young.

00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:42.700
But now I can honestly say, we built
a new building a few years ago and

00:39:42.700 --> 00:39:47.260
I said, you know, as long as health
permits, I've enjoyed this so

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:52.060
much and the area I'm working on keeps
changing every few years because

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:55.270
the nature of venture capital and
technology.

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:57.502
I'm gonna do this for
the rest of my life but

00:39:57.502 --> 00:40:00.550
only do things that actually
make a positive difference.

00:40:00.550 --> 00:40:05.125
But then also, anytime you make
a difference, you make money and I think

00:40:05.125 --> 00:40:10.675
when you make money as a consequence of
trying to do really interesting companies,

00:40:10.675 --> 00:40:14.725
you make much more of an impact
then if you do it as a transaction,

00:40:14.725 --> 00:40:17.210
buy/sell kind of thing.

00:40:17.210 --> 00:40:20.390
In fact, if I get a plan,
business plan that talks about

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:24.250
exit in the first couple of pages,
I almost never read the rest of it.

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:27.750
&gt;&gt; It's a good tip, right?

00:40:27.750 --> 00:40:28.320
&gt;&gt; Can you hear me?
Yeah?

00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:33.850
So I'm Melina and I actually work for
a healthcare start up.

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:36.310
I have a pointed question about you.

00:40:36.310 --> 00:40:40.200
I'm very impressed by
the confidence you exhibit and

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:42.280
obviously you have reasons
to be that confident.

00:40:42.280 --> 00:40:43.816
&gt;&gt; Some people call it arrogance.

00:40:43.816 --> 00:40:47.260
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My question is did you,

00:40:47.260 --> 00:40:52.260
when you were a kid, did you get
support from your family that made

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:53.890
you as confident as you are right now?

00:40:55.330 --> 00:40:58.680
&gt;&gt; You know, e, e,
first it's hard to remember.

00:41:00.670 --> 00:41:02.230
I always did my thing.

00:41:02.230 --> 00:41:06.166
I always did things most kids didn't do.

00:41:06.166 --> 00:41:11.196
And my parents always sort

00:41:11.196 --> 00:41:15.840
of, I guess support is
probably the right word.

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:17.860
Indian families are very different, you

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:25.115
never did things your parents really
oppose, but, I tended to do some of that.

00:41:25.115 --> 00:41:30.560
[LAUGH] but I always had great dialog with
my parents, and they actually sort of,

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:33.990
accepted me very early doing,
going out of the bounds.

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:41.159
For those of you interested in my attitude
and those of you who may have kids,

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:49.120
I was asked to give a talk at Harker
School here in, you know, in Saratoga.

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:53.260
So give a talk to the high school kids,
and I did a presentation.

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:59.235
My first slide was something like why
you should only color outside the lines.

00:41:59.235 --> 00:42:02.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My second slide was about

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:05.305
why you should disregard your teachers.

00:42:05.305 --> 00:42:08.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My third one was why you should disobey

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:09.158
parents.

00:42:09.158 --> 00:42:13.574
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Eh, and, and yeah, it was sort of funny

00:42:13.574 --> 00:42:18.400
and the kids loved it, but
I'm dead serious about it.

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:20.260
I am dead serious about it.

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:27.320
I actually think,
inculcating an attitude of exploration,

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:31.460
not confirmation to a bound set,
a bound set by others,

00:42:31.460 --> 00:42:36.979
is what creates this internal compass and
belief system and confidence in yourself.

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:42.930
Again I say,
90% of you will do what's expected.

00:42:42.930 --> 00:42:46.964
You know, after this you'll be
motivated and then go back and say, oh,

00:42:46.964 --> 00:42:48.827
that Goldman job, it pays more.

00:42:53.597 --> 00:42:54.097
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:42:57.200 --> 00:43:01.100
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Neil MBA1,
thanks for your speech.

00:43:01.100 --> 00:43:06.163
I just on the end of this thing that you
mentioned, there's the VC field now,

00:43:06.163 --> 00:43:07.505
as compared to in the past,

00:43:07.505 --> 00:43:12.270
has become a lot more crowded and
there's a talk of tech bubble here.

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:14.660
In your opinion,
are we in the middle of a bubble?

00:43:14.660 --> 00:43:16.700
And if so,
when do you foresee it bursting?

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:17.200
Thanks.

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:23.630
&gt;&gt; I don't,
I know that I don't know the answer.

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:28.015
I also know anybody who tells you they
know the answer is probably stupid.

00:43:28.015 --> 00:43:32.115
[LAUGH].

00:43:32.115 --> 00:43:36.030
[LAUGH].

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:39.365
&gt;&gt; So yeah, thanks, thanks for
your talk, Pablo Fuentes, MBA10.

00:43:40.380 --> 00:43:44.820
So I think your brand of swashbuckling is
particularly effective if you show some

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:45.500
vulnerability.

00:43:45.500 --> 00:43:48.950
So I'm gonna be your buddy and allow you
to tell us a story about a time when you

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:51.710
got crushed by somebody's brutal
honesty and what you did with that.

00:43:53.360 --> 00:43:59.680
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
So,

00:43:59.680 --> 00:44:03.680
look it would take an hour to
talk about all my mistakes.

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:06.480
I actually let me put it this way.

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:15.040
In the last 15 years, there's only once
in my life I've called somebody and

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:16.810
say I'd like to speak at this conference.

00:44:18.450 --> 00:44:20.140
And that wasn't a popular conference.

00:44:20.140 --> 00:44:23.010
It was a small thing in a hotel
room in San Francisco and

00:44:23.010 --> 00:44:27.670
was called FailureCon where
everybody who had co, failed came.

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.200
And I wanted to speak to
this issue of failure.

00:44:33.256 --> 00:44:38.610
There are so many s, stupid things.

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:41.160
You know, the iPad is so popular.

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:47.130
We tried to do it, probably one of
my large mistakes, in the late 80s.

00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:52.600
And I could go into details,
and I, I'll cut it short.

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:58.600
What I would say is I'm never
embarrassed about my failures.

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:03.280
And so if you search in the web for
my failures, you'll find lots of them and

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:05.600
you'll find me talking about them.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:08.558
And it's important because,
given my philosophy,

00:45:08.558 --> 00:45:12.940
where I screw up doesn't matter,
because it's a long list.

00:45:12.940 --> 00:45:16.380
In fact,
it's a longer list than my successes.

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:18.130
But like I started with,

00:45:18.130 --> 00:45:22.920
nobody remembers the data dump here,
everybody remembers Sun.

00:45:24.550 --> 00:45:25.050
Right?

00:45:26.710 --> 00:45:31.330
The number of venture companies
where I made stupid mistakes.

00:45:31.330 --> 00:45:34.720
I'll, I'll tell you one of my,
big failures.

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:36.258
So if you go back to Sun.

00:45:36.258 --> 00:45:38.661
1980s.

00:45:38.661 --> 00:45:41.560
Sun's logo, or,

00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:46.290
on, on, le, motto under the logo
was the network is the computer.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:51.920
And these couple of people started
this software program that did

00:45:51.920 --> 00:45:54.510
routing of IP packets of network traffic.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:59.802
That happen to be the beginning of Cisco.

00:45:59.802 --> 00:46:05.083
Now, when you say the network
is the computer and make these

00:46:05.083 --> 00:46:11.240
arrogant marketing lines, and you
forget that routing is the key element.

00:46:11.240 --> 00:46:16.050
And Cisco was born as an application
on Sun, and we didn't see it.

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:21.600
And I said to myself, duh, how stupid was
I not to see it when it was happening?

00:46:21.600 --> 00:46:23.120
Even when I saw the application!

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:26.848
A router was an application
on a sun machine,

00:46:26.848 --> 00:46:29.352
we missed the largest
opportunity Sun put out.

00:46:29.352 --> 00:46:34.295
I can find 100 such examples,
the point is,

00:46:34.295 --> 00:46:38.010
try and fail but don't fail to try.

00:46:41.660 --> 00:46:45.690
My, a couple of other favorite
quotes along those lines,

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:47.340
since we're coming to the end of our time.

00:46:49.070 --> 00:46:50.610
Robert F.
Kennedy said,

00:46:50.610 --> 00:46:53.980
only those who fail greatly
can succeed greatly.

00:46:55.790 --> 00:46:58.690
The classic example of that is Elon Musk.

00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:05.030
He said forget this bullshit
about supplying batteries to GM.

00:47:07.340 --> 00:47:09.660
They're just too slow
to make change happen.

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:15.950
You know I was talking about retailing
Amazon not Walmart, YouTube not NBC.

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:19.530
Think about GM or Tesla.

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:22.620
GM spent way more money than Tesla did.

00:47:22.620 --> 00:47:27.440
Space, NASA and Lockheed spent
way more money than SpaceX did.

00:47:27.440 --> 00:47:30.110
I can go on and on, every major change.

00:47:33.720 --> 00:47:38.590
So failure, try and fail,
but don't fail to try.

00:47:39.694 --> 00:47:44.565
Nassim Taleb and his book Antifragile
which I recommend everybody read if they

00:47:44.565 --> 00:47:49.581
are setting off on a career as a personal
philosophy, mine happens to coincide,

00:47:49.581 --> 00:47:54.464
and I'm not talking about Black Swan,
but his book called Antifragile.

00:47:54.464 --> 00:47:58.316
I always love ending with a good rec,
book recommendation.

00:47:58.316 --> 00:48:00.643
I think that's important, so.

00:48:00.643 --> 00:48:04.149
I'm just not embarrassed
about my stupid mistakes.

00:48:04.149 --> 00:48:07.315
I mean, I think that's the difference.

00:48:07.315 --> 00:48:11.005
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; I'd know if I answered your question.

00:48:11.005 --> 00:48:15.115
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; When did I get crushed?

00:48:15.115 --> 00:48:18.930
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; By brutal honesty.

00:48:18.930 --> 00:48:20.599
I mean, I've offended plenty of people.

00:48:22.162 --> 00:48:28.120
And that, so I won't go into
the details because the public company,

00:48:28.120 --> 00:48:34.540
two public companies involved but,
I was on a, on a panel with the CEO

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:39.350
of this public company and I basically
told them how stupid they were and then

00:48:39.350 --> 00:48:43.530
there was payback in a indirect way and
that really hurt this other company and

00:48:43.530 --> 00:48:48.300
it you know, literally killed,
almost killed the company.

00:48:49.990 --> 00:48:53.280
Unfortunately because they're public
companies I won't go into details.

00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:56.130
So that's an example where
brutal honesty really hurt me.

00:49:00.390 --> 00:49:04.530
I can think of lots of, look, there's
plenty of people in the VC business

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:09.100
that don't like me because I tell them
they, they, they're sort of worthless.

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:12.170
Eh, eh, I do!

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:14.320
But again it's an indulgence on my part.

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:17.170
The really good people
I don't say that to.

00:49:17.170 --> 00:49:20.550
If I don't know somebody enough
I won't say to that to them.

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:23.540
But if I do know for
sure I do make sure I tell them.

00:49:23.540 --> 00:49:28.220
More importantly, I tell [INAUDIBLE] knows
what I think and they often tell the VC.

00:49:28.220 --> 00:49:29.930
But it's part of life.

00:49:29.930 --> 00:49:31.965
&gt;&gt; Now I want to make sure we
have time for one more question.

00:49:31.965 --> 00:49:32.630
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:49:32.630 --> 00:49:34.564
&gt;&gt; Actually,
a quick announcement before that.

00:49:34.564 --> 00:49:39.090
Friends of Ronid from the class of 2010
are welcome to join his family backstage

00:49:39.090 --> 00:49:41.379
after the event ends to share stories and
reminisce.

00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:45.820
So, this questions has sort of sort
of a view from the top tradition.

00:49:45.820 --> 00:49:50.000
And every applicant to Stanford GSB is
asked this question on their application.

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:53.010
However, you were not asked
this question in 1978,

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:56.100
because this question didn't exist
in 1978, it started in 2000s.

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:59.520
So here's your chance to
answer this question,

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:00.920
what matters most to you and why?

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:06.820
&gt;&gt; You know I think it sort of and,
and I won't phrase this in,

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:11.680
in a do gooder kind of way and
I don't mean it that way.

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:15.390
I just find it's cool to
disrupt things for the better.

00:50:16.630 --> 00:50:19.040
And so when I say impact

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:22.810
I sort of feel like I'm being
a troll on various industries.

00:50:22.810 --> 00:50:26.870
It's a luxury I've earned to, because
I don't need to pay my mortgage and

00:50:26.870 --> 00:50:29.150
so, I like taking on new things.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:33.080
You know, I, it's sort of like
now people are mad at me for

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:34.490
saying we don't need doctors.

00:50:34.490 --> 00:50:36.880
I actually don't believe we do.

00:50:36.880 --> 00:50:41.620
So but we need, a different kind of
doctor to do a different kind of thing.

00:50:41.620 --> 00:50:45.570
And I wouldn't go to Stanford Med
school to get the best doctors.

00:50:45.570 --> 00:50:50.160
I'd go to UCLA film school because the
human element of care is important, and

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:52.370
acting becomes important.

00:50:52.370 --> 00:50:55.430
And that's where most of our doctors
will come from in 15 years for

00:50:55.430 --> 00:50:57.340
the human element of care.

00:50:57.340 --> 00:51:02.650
And people hate it, doctors hate
it when I say to get the most,

00:51:02.650 --> 00:51:05.920
most of the human element of care
you need the most humane humans and

00:51:05.920 --> 00:51:13.737
those aren't the high-IQ people who
got into Stanford Med School, reality.

00:51:13.737 --> 00:51:18.180
But what I would say is knowing I

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:23.200
can make a difference in industries where
things are positive is very rewarding.

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:28.200
And let me end on the note that
I actually like working on

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:32.740
things that my four kids would be
proud of me working on, right.

00:51:32.740 --> 00:51:35.670
And there's this question that
all of you will face of work,

00:51:35.670 --> 00:51:38.420
life balance so let me end on that note.

00:51:42.620 --> 00:51:47.520
Working on cool things excites me,
keeps me motivated, I like to say,

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:54.520
you you grow old when you retire, not you
re, you don't retire when you grow old.

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:59.610
And as long as you are stimulated and
excited and whatever your passion is, and

00:51:59.610 --> 00:52:03.388
it can be what I do,
it could be I want to train a whale.

00:52:04.920 --> 00:52:07.440
I find that a really hard task.

00:52:07.440 --> 00:52:09.550
It's a significant challenge by itself.

00:52:09.550 --> 00:52:12.490
Maybe it's not hugely impactful for
society.

00:52:12.490 --> 00:52:17.050
But I actually love that example of,
if I got passionate about that and

00:52:17.050 --> 00:52:19.650
did that, and
it's a really hard thing to do.

00:52:20.990 --> 00:52:23.010
It'd be exciting to go pursue it.

00:52:23.010 --> 00:52:27.450
So I like hard challenges, I like
difficult things, I like working against

00:52:27.450 --> 00:52:33.100
the odds and that brings out
my competitive spirit and now

00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:37.400
I happen to constrain it to areas where
I think I can have a positive impact.

00:52:37.400 --> 00:52:40.640
But not so much in the do-gooder thing,
it's a luxury I can indulge in.

00:52:40.640 --> 00:52:42.520
It makes my kids feel better.

00:52:42.520 --> 00:52:43.940
So let me end there.

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:49.350
Through all of the 90s when my
kids were young, I had one rule.

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:53.810
This 15 minute thing,
managing my time came from the following.

00:52:54.870 --> 00:53:00.150
I realized it was easy for
all of us to say things and

00:53:00.150 --> 00:53:03.678
then, and mean them and
not stay true to them.

00:53:03.678 --> 00:53:06.155
The most important?

00:53:06.155 --> 00:53:07.300
Family.

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:12.430
Everybody says the right words,
they don't live by them.

00:53:12.430 --> 00:53:16.220
So, what I decided I would
do is get a monthly report

00:53:16.220 --> 00:53:20.360
of every category of time I spent time on.

00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:25.170
It could be working with entrepreneurs,
evaluating new companies,

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:29.690
being polite talking to journalists,
being polite because somebody said,

00:53:29.690 --> 00:53:33.350
here my son wants to talk,
have a conversation.

00:53:33.350 --> 00:53:38.120
I classified my time into 20 categories,
one of the most critical

00:53:38.120 --> 00:53:42.920
lines from that was the number of times
I had dinner with my kids at home.

00:53:44.170 --> 00:53:48.160
And I set an impossible goal of 25 times,
a month.

00:53:49.970 --> 00:53:53.080
And, it took me a year or
so, but eventually I met it.

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:58.060
But more importantly, every month I had
to report on whether I did or didn't.

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:02.860
I do believe work,
life balance is possible.

00:54:02.860 --> 00:54:07.710
You just have to be extremely
disciplined about it.

00:54:07.710 --> 00:54:12.300
I no longer went to the pub,
right, or the.

00:54:12.300 --> 00:54:17.000
I know no longer did
baseball games with friends,

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:19.380
I only did it with my kids or son.

00:54:20.510 --> 00:54:24.750
I, I decided what my
priorities were explicitly and

00:54:24.750 --> 00:54:27.480
then measured it to meet the requirements.

00:54:27.480 --> 00:54:32.190
I highly recommend you come up with
your own priorities and measure them.

00:54:32.190 --> 00:54:33.280
Thank you all very much.

00:54:33.280 --> 00:54:39.440
&gt;&gt; Thank you Rao thank you
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:54:39.440 --> 00:54:44.260
[MUSIC]

